# sys_write.tkr — the §16 second-crumb fixture. `main.tks` bump-allocates a fresh NUL-terminated
# copy of "hi\n" via `teko::mem::as_cstr`, bridges its opaque address to an `i64` word via
# `teko::sys::ptr_word`, and issues `teko::sys::syscall3(sys::SYS_WRITE, 1, <word>, 3)` — the raw
# x86_64 Linux `write(1, buf, 3)` syscall — writing "hi\n" to stdout before exiting 0. The harness
# observes exit code 0, proving the `syscall3` intrinsic plus the `ptr_word` bridge (§1.2) issued
# the real instruction and the kernel honoured it (the emitted "hi" on stdout is the bonus proof a
# human/CI log can eyeball).

Feature: sys_write — the §16 ptr_word bridge + syscall3 intrinsic issue x86_64 Linux write(1, buf, 3)

  Scenario: raw_write_prints_and_exits_zero — the process writes "hi\n" to stdout and exits 0
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
